Using `block` in everyday programs.

No longer reserved for pipeline automation, `block` and "tool-native error
reporting" are now available for any program. It is important to please keep
the clever to a minimum. We are not going to use `throw`. There is no point to
it when you can just use `abend` and `throw` should be removed.

Almost removed the `-v` option to `try` when I saw it, and certainly it was
marked for disposal, but then I set out to actually write something with this
new universal `block` and realized I'm no longer evaluating code as I do with
`show` in the automation pipelines. I needed that file handle in order to
capture errors when doing command substitution.

```
try -v try && body=(git log -n 1 --format=%B 2>&$try) || tried
```

The whole point of this exercise was trying to find something less verbose
than the `pocket` pattern that has emerged.

```
if ! pocket -s out err git log -n 1 --format=%B; then
    abend -n $err 'fatal: git error: unable to get commit body'
fi
```

While `try -v try &&` had bothered me in the past, it now looks reasonable in
part because file handles and redirection look reasonable to me now that time
has passed and in part because repetition will make it filter out.

Nice thing about `try -v try` is that you can put entire pipelines inside a
`{}` and `{} 2>&$try' and have a more generic message, not have to go step by
step as with `pocket` and not have to skip on error reporting, using `pocket`
for a call to `gh` but skipping it for the `jq` manipulation. The `gh | jq` is
really one operation, because if `jq` fails its 99% of the time because `gh`
or whatever utility returned unfamiliar JSON, probably accompanied by an error
on standard error.

Also, do note that syntax errors or other errors coming out of Zsh, well, I'm
not sure what path those follow. We could write a simple test where we assign
to a variable marked read-only within a `{}` block and see what happens with
the resulting throw. We can reason that in command substitution it will work
fine, and the only other way to get that to `try`, not a command but an
assignment, would be with `eval`, so `try || tried` will likely work for any
sort of error and capture any sort of standard error message.

Ah, but what about...

```
try -v try && { readonly x; x=; } 2>&$try || tried
```

Note that will `try || tried`, like with pipeline automations using `show` you
are going to have to do things step by step, generally breaking up your
program into reportable steps, which you do anyway, but I'm curious to see
if you do it more. What will code look like if this becomes a once and for all
pattern?
